  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199831/assets/e4a79a8b0eb2c0c79b1cdcb0623dc913169db8a46aab9b0a18c23f96ac61a0cd.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $P$ (other than $P$ itself)?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $P$ is a kite with a pair of opposite congruent angles and $2$ pairs of congruent sides. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $P$ because $R$ is not a kite.\n\n- $S$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $S$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2.5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n- $T$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $T$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{2.5}=\\frac{5}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only\".",
